WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.940
One of my first reactions was pure terror that&nbsp;
because I was so excited I just became paranoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.680
that I was going to um somehow blurt out that&nbsp;
I refuse the Fields medal when obviously I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:16.020
wanted to accept it. So I think I had the most&nbsp;
bizarre response to his question that I very very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.020 --> 00:00:20.520
slowly and deliberately said, yes I accept the&nbsp;
Fields medal which is the most surreal phrase.

00:00:24.600 --> 00:00:30.540
So certainly when I was in secondary school I&nbsp;
was aware of the concept of the Fields medal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:36.540
as a sort of maths version of the Nobel prize&nbsp;
or something. I think the Fields model was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.540 --> 00:00:40.800
only maths prize I was aware of then. I can't&nbsp;
remember at all when the first time I'd heard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:45.960
about the Fields medal would be but yeah it must&nbsp;
have been at some point when I was at school.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.980
(Brady: Not Goodwill Hunting? That that's when I first heard of&nbsp;
it. Goodwill Hunting made the Fields medal famous)&nbsp;

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:55.200
- Following you around going 'the Fields medal, the Fields medal'
- It's about my medal isn't it? Oh God I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:59.580
go home and get it for you, you can have it.
- Yeah so&nbsp;it took me an embarrassingly long period of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:05.280
to watch Goodwill Hunting I think. So um despite&nbsp;
the fact I've been keen on maths for such a long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.360
time yeah somehow everyone else seemed to have&nbsp;
watched Goodwill Hunting but I hadn't watched it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:13.980
so I didn't learn about it from there.
- (So it's well&nbsp;documented, we've even spoke about it. You started)

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:18.900
(having some successes and making some impressive&nbsp;
breakthroughs; and people started talking about)

00:01:18.900 --> 00:01:23.460
(you being a contender for the Fields medal. I&nbsp;
used to see on Reddit every time Fields medal)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:29.520
(season came around your name would be amongst the&nbsp;
possible winners. Did people ever talk to you about)&nbsp;

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.060
(it? Were people- did people ever say to you,&nbsp;
'James I reckon you could win it this year', like)&nbsp;

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:38.400
(things like that. Were you very aware of it?)
- So&nbsp;maybe a couple of colleagues heard loose comments&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:45.660
uh and so you made indications that they thought&nbsp;
it was possible. But um strangely the biggest thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:49.320
was that people didn't talk about it. That normally&nbsp;
mathematicians like to gossip a little bit about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:55.740
Fields medals and things um and so in some cycles&nbsp;
sort of when we're just having uh dinner or if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.740 --> 00:02:00.960
we're having coffee or something together people&nbsp;
gossip about who might win a Fields medal. But it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.960 --> 00:02:06.180
was notable that for this cycle no one was talking&nbsp;
to me about the Fields medal at all and I seemed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.180 --> 00:02:10.560
to be cut out of all the normal gossiping that&nbsp;
was going on. So in terms of my interactions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:16.020
with colleagues it was actually more than not&nbsp;
talking to me than them talking to me that uh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:21.420
was an indication that they thought maybe I was&nbsp;
one of the candidates.
- (How did you find out?)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:29.160
So I was decorating my house, so I was at the top of&nbsp;a 
ladder painting one of the ceilings and I came&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:34.800
down to have a break and I checked my phone and&nbsp;
then I had an email from the IMU president saying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.400 --> 00:02:41.520
'could we arrange a zoom call sometime soon'. And&nbsp;
I had a suspicion then as to what it was. I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.520 --> 00:02:46.260
desperately trying to convince myself that&nbsp;
he wanted me to be on some boring committee&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.260 --> 00:02:51.420
and this was nothing to get excited about at all&nbsp;
because I didn't want to get ahead of myself.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:56.160
But realistically that's when I had the first sort&nbsp;
of inclination that maybe I'd won the Fields medal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:02.100
and then we organised this Zoom call in my office.
And fortunately he was very kind and just said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:06.960
basically straight up, oh we've decided to award&nbsp;
you the Fields medal. So it cut all the tension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.960 --> 00:03:11.280
out pretty quickly. 
- (What was your first feeling?&nbsp;Do you remember what first went through your head?)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:19.860
Um obviously I was ecstatic. The IMU president asks&nbsp;
you um will you accept the Fields medal - because I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:25.020
guess there was this notable case with Perelman
that he declined the Fields medal. So in fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:32.040
one of my first reactions was pure terror that&nbsp;
because I was so excited I just became paranoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.660
that I was going to somehow blurt out that&nbsp;
I refuse the Fields medal when obviously&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:42.120
I wanted to accept it. So I think I had the most&nbsp;
bizarre response to his question that I very very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:46.680
slowly and deliberately said 'yes I accept the&nbsp;
Fields medal' which is the most surreal phrase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:51.780
um that I think I said because I was just&nbsp;
completely terrified that somehow I'd been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.780 --> 00:03:54.840
awarded the Fields medal and I would&nbsp;
accidentally reject the Fields medal.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.800 --> 00:04:02.280
(Okay - well done for not rejecting it! What's&nbsp;
the chat like then? Does he tell you- obviously)&nbsp;

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.000
(he tells you some procedural things; are you&nbsp;
sworn to secrecy? What's the deal at this point?)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:13.500
Um yeah they give you- um he said that he'd talk
me through a few of the basic procedural things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:19.680
and then there's a number of extra details&nbsp;
that go through. They strongly ask you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:24.060
to keep it secret- they understand&nbsp;
that you'll tell a couple of very close&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.840 --> 00:04:30.720
um family members and maybe a couple of close&nbsp;
colleagues um but uh they're very very keen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:30.720 --> 00:04:36.480
keep it as secret as possible because naturally&nbsp;
that's part of the um fun of all of these awards. &nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:40.740
(The Fields medal- there's obviously other people who win&nbsp;
the Fields medal; do they tell you the other)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:45.240
(winners at that point? Or do you not know who&nbsp;
your sort of colleagues are at that stage?)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.240 --> 00:04:51.480
No so I only knew for sure who the other winners&nbsp;
were when I walked into the backstage room in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:56.940
Helsinki right before the Fields medal ceremony. I&nbsp;
was certainly trying to sort of guess from other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.940 --> 00:05:02.100
things and you talk to some journalists. So&nbsp;
I think I had heard from one of the journalists&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.100 --> 00:05:05.940
that I was the youngest Fields medalist because&nbsp;
the journalists obviously know the other ones.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.660 --> 00:05:11.940
But yeah I only knew for sure who the other&nbsp;
ones were when I was backstage and I recognised&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:21.240
the other people. 
- (So when you got off that call&nbsp;who did you tell?)
- Funnily enough this was in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.240 --> 00:05:28.440
early evening and I'd arranged to meet a group of&nbsp;
mathematicians in the pub. And so I went straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:33.360
off to the pub because I was already a bit late to&nbsp;
meet them but I had to keep myself very quiet and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:38.460
not tell anyone. So I had this very awkward period&nbsp;
of like socialising with some other mathematicians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.460 --> 00:05:43.080
and just having chats about different things-
- (So&nbsp;they're like 'why are you late James?' And you're)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.080 --> 00:05:47.580
(like 'oh sorry, I was just winning a Fields&nbsp;medal')
- Yeah so I made up some rubbish excuse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:47.580 --> 00:05:51.180
about like getting distracted and getting&nbsp;
too into my maths and something running over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:57.300
um and I was desperately trying to sort of not&nbsp;
have a big beaming smile the entire time and to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.300 --> 00:06:01.920
just talk about other things. So the Fields medal&nbsp;
is always normally given at the International&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:09.000
Congress of Mathematicians. This was uh due to&nbsp;
be held in Russia um in Saint Petersburg but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:15.000
because of the war in Ukraine it was decided&nbsp;
that this was not feasible for obvious reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.000 --> 00:06:21.360
and so there was a somewhat last minute switch to&nbsp;
Helsinki. And most of the International Congress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.360 --> 00:06:27.900
was then held online but they had an in-person&nbsp;
ceremony for the awarding of the Fields medals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.500 --> 00:06:34.200
in Helsinki along with the initial talks and&nbsp;
the opening ceremony. 
- (You go backstage - what, to some)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.200 --> 00:06:37.440
(green room or something - and that's when you&nbsp;
first see the other winners?)
- Yeah that's right.

00:06:38.100 --> 00:06:44.220
Um uh yeah so I'm there in my suit feeling&nbsp;
super nervous and I go backstage and there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:49.800
all the other winners and um I think everyone is&nbsp;
sort of super nervous and being a bit quiet and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.800 --> 00:06:56.880
then uh Hugo Dominil-Copin comes in and he's&nbsp;
exceptionally lively and he starts chatting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:59.760
to people and he breaks the ice and then&nbsp;
suddenly everyone's chatting to everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:04.200
else and I think that was great because it&nbsp;
calmed my nerves an awful lot.
- (Did you know the)

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:11.520
(three others? Like to recognise them or?)
- Um I&nbsp;visually recognised them yeah. So I had maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:17.820
seen one or two of them before in person but&nbsp;
I basically hadn't seen them in person and I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.820 --> 00:07:23.460
didn't know them personally but I knew who they&nbsp;
were and I recognised them from photos I'd seen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.460 --> 00:07:30.660
(Did you know any much about their mathematics?)
- Um I&nbsp;knew a little bit about uh most of their mathematics&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.920
yeah. I'd heard about some of the sort of main&nbsp;
results that they'd had.
- (I sometimes look at)&nbsp;

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:38.820
(these lists of four yearly winners as these&nbsp;
clusters and I always think you're kind of- you're)&nbsp;

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:43.740
(you're all bonded now aren't you? Like that you're-
you're a little class. Do you feel a bond with them?)&nbsp;

00:07:45.060 --> 00:07:50.760
Um I certainly feel a bit of a bond with them,&nbsp;
yeah. I mean it's not like we've had any uh extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.760 --> 00:07:56.160
community - it's not- we don't have a 2022 Fields&nbsp;
medalists WhatsApp group or anything like that.

00:07:56.820 --> 00:08:01.620
But uh yeah there's a certain&nbsp;
amount of camaraderie that uh you know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:06.060
it was a very important time in my life and they&nbsp;
were all going through exactly the same thing at

00:08:06.060 --> 00:08:10.200
exactly the same time.
- (Brilliant. And what happens&nbsp;
at the ceremony? Did you have to make a speech? Do)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.200 --> 00:08:14.520
(you just take your medal and walk off the stage?&nbsp;
How does the procedure go?)
- So there's the award&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.520 --> 00:08:21.480
ceremony initially where I just take my medal. Then&nbsp;
there's another distinguished mathematician in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.480 --> 00:08:28.500
area who gives a sort of presentation on each of&nbsp;
the winner's works but that's all I have to do for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:33.840
um that first day. And then in the second day I&nbsp;
give a lecture associated to winning the Fields&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.840 --> 00:08:37.920
medal. 
- (So then you've got it, you know, you bring&nbsp;
it home - we'll look at it in a minute hopefully.)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.920 --> 00:08:45.480
(There it is, I can't wait to see it. But um like&nbsp;
what next? Like it's kind of like- I know there)&nbsp;

00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.560
(are some other big prizes in mathematics now,&nbsp;
like big big prizes but the Fields medal still)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:56.280
(seems to be like the Nobel prize-y one. Is there&nbsp;
is there a feeling at all of 'hmm now what? Like)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.280 --> 00:09:01.140
(that's like- that this could be the biggest thing&nbsp;
I ever win!)
- Yeah it's only downhill from here.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.860 --> 00:09:09.180
Um I mean I think most mathematicians are&nbsp;
kind of really motivated by mathematics. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:16.140
um I'm completely ecstatic and overjoyed that I've&nbsp;
won the Fields medal uh but you know the thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.140 --> 00:09:22.680
that's always driven me is understanding kind of&nbsp;
truth in mathematics and getting to the heart of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:30.900
um kind of mathematical puzzles and things&nbsp;
like that. Um and the sort of real satisfaction&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:37.980
um is from when I prove a theorem that I really&nbsp;
like, I get this immense joy from that. And so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.980 --> 00:09:42.240
that's still what drives me on and that's what&nbsp;
drove me initially and that hasn't changed at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.240 --> 00:09:48.780
all. So um I hope that I can keep my feet on&nbsp;
the ground and not get too full of myself or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.780 --> 00:09:55.140
anything like that, and to just continue being a&nbsp;
research mathematician who's driven by trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.140 --> 00:10:00.000
understand research mathematics. Yeah so the&nbsp;
Fields medal is only awarded once every four&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.000 --> 00:10:05.460
years and it's only awarded to mathematicians&nbsp;
who are under the age of 40. So it's very much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.460 --> 00:10:12.660
only given to young mathematicians. I think&nbsp;
originally this was intended to be a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:20.460
prize for promising young mathematicians as an&nbsp;
encouragement but just for historical reasons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.900 --> 00:10:27.000
the people who've won it has led to becoming a&nbsp;
much more prestigious award than maybe it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.000 --> 00:10:34.620
ever originally intended to be. And so there's now&nbsp;
this odd status that one- at least one of the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:34.620 --> 00:10:40.740
premier prizes in mathematics has this very&nbsp;
young age limit. Yeah I feel that there's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.740 --> 00:10:48.360
obvious benefits and disadvantages that um on&nbsp;
some level I sometimes find it a little bit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:53.280
dissatisfying that Nobel prizes&nbsp;
are given to people really right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.280 --> 00:10:57.180
at the end of their career; often they've&nbsp;
been retired for a long period of time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.920 --> 00:11:04.380
and so it's not selecting the sort of most&nbsp;
exciting work and the people who you're expecting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:10.020
to come up with the next breakthroughs. And that's&nbsp;
one nice thing about the Fields medal, that it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.620 --> 00:11:18.120
choosing very promising and talented young&nbsp;
mathematicians um and people who are therefore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:23.880
maybe amongst the people who might be making the&nbsp;
next set of breakthroughs. 
- (You can- and you can)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:27.780
(use the benefit of it - some of those little doors&nbsp;
it opens and advantages it gives you, you can use)&nbsp;

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:33.360
(while you're still practicing.)
- Uh yeah exactly.&nbsp;
So that's certainly one benefit of the Fields

00:11:33.360 --> 00:11:42.420
medal. Obviously a big disadvantage is that the&nbsp;
relatively low age limit has a certain distorting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.420 --> 00:11:50.160
effect that different education systems accelerate&nbsp;
people to different extents and so a 40 year&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:56.760
cutoff is quite arbitrary. People who have to&nbsp;
do military service are at disadvantages; people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:02.520
who maybe take career breaks because of caring&nbsp;
responsibilities or having children or things like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.520 --> 00:12:09.060
that it sort of slightly unfairly disadvantages.&nbsp;
The fact that it's just once every four years with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:17.700
this age limit means that uh what year you're born&nbsp;
mod 4 has a advantage as to whether you have more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.700 --> 00:12:23.040
possible research time to make contributions or&nbsp;
not. So it has lots of sort of slightly undesirable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.040 --> 00:12:28.980
effects like this as well.
- (Yeah if you solve the&nbsp;
Riemann hypothesis as a 41 year old - too bad)&nbsp;

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:33.960
Yeah exactly but if you'd somehow just been born one&nbsp;
year earlier then everything would have been fine.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.620 --> 00:12:41.100
(Can I please see the medal?)
- You can indeed. So this&nbsp;is my medal.
- (It comes in that case does it?)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.100 --> 00:12:45.480
Yeah it came in this case. 
- (What was it like when they&nbsp;
handed it to you? Was it- what did that feel like?)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:46.680 --> 00:12:51.540
Um I mean I think I was in a daze the&nbsp;
entire time uh through the ceremony so&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.200 --> 00:12:57.180
um everything felt amazing and I was just on&nbsp;
cloud nine but I was also slightly unaware of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.180 --> 00:13:03.060
everything that was going on.
- (Did any of your&nbsp;family come over to Helsinki?)
- No, so the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.960 --> 00:13:12.960
strange thing was that um I was awarded my medal&nbsp;
on the 5th of July but my first son was going to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.960 --> 00:13:20.820
be born on the 7th of July. So um I received the&nbsp;
medal on the 5th; on the morning of the 6th I gave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.820 --> 00:13:26.220
my talk associated to the Fields medal. Normally&nbsp;
they give the talks in alphabetical order but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.220 --> 00:13:32.100
they changed the order because I needed to get&nbsp;
back to the UK from Finland. And I flew straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.100 --> 00:13:38.880
back to the UK after giving the first talk in&nbsp;
the morning um and then in the morning of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.880 --> 00:13:44.820
7th of July my first son was born. So that was a-&nbsp;
it's going to be difficult to top that week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.440 --> 00:13:52.080
(Yeah, brilliant. And the last question I have is what&nbsp;
do you do with it? Like where like- what do you)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:52.080 --> 00:13:56.340
(do with the medal? Do you ever look at it? Does it&nbsp;
have pride of place? Is it locked in a safe or)&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.340 --> 00:14:01.020
(like- you know as much as you can tell us.)
- Yeah so&nbsp;this is one of the slightly disappointing things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:08.040
I guess that because it is sort of valuable,&nbsp;
both sentimentally but also I think the medal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.040 --> 00:14:14.520
itself is gold and so has some value, I&nbsp;
sort of have it locked in a box at home which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:18.540
means I don't actually see it very often, it's&nbsp;
just sort of locked in a cupboard somewhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:18.540 --> 00:14:22.500
And so in some ways it's a bit silly that&nbsp;
I win this medal that I'm super proud of,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.500 --> 00:14:26.760
I really like, but rather than show it off or&nbsp;
look at it it just gets put in the cupboard.&nbsp;&nbsp;

